<header><h1>Coexistence: Proof</h1></header>
<article>
<p>The source text behind each note on <a href="/R_coexistence">Coexistence</a>. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>

<h2>Notes</h2>
    <ol class="note">
    <li>Some game effects allow two or more players&rsquo; units to exist on a planet without triggering combat; this is coexistence.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;Some game effects allow a player&rsquo;s units to coexist with other units on a planet. When a unit or units coexist with another player&rsquo;s units, they do not trigger combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>One player controls the planet; all other players are coexisting on that planet.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;The player whose units triggered coexistence does not gain or retain control of the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol>
        <li>If a game effect instructs a player to coexist on a planet they do not control, the original controller of that planet retains control.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;The player whose units triggered coexistence does not gain or retain control of the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>If a game effect instructs a player to coexist on a planet they do control, then another player will gain control of that planet (which will exhaust that planet).<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;if they already controlled it, the player they are now coexisting with gains control of it instead, exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if they held it, they lose control&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
    <li>If multiple players may choose to coexist on a planet during the same timing window, the active player chooses first; if they decline, the other player may then choose to coexist.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What if a planet owned by DWS gets invaded by the Firmament mechs? &hellip; A: Both coexistences happen in the same timing window, so it will go by active player order, the active player is given the opportunity to coexist first, and if they decline, then the other player may choose to coexist.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If additional units are added to those already coexisting, by, for example, committing additional ground forces during an invasion, or resolving an effect that allows a unit to be placed directly on a planet, the added units immediately coexist.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;If the coexisting player places, produces, or moves additional units onto that planet, those units also coexist.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Structures without ground forces may coexist on a planet by themselves, and may maintain control of a planet while another player&rsquo;s ground forces coexist on it.<sup>9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence and Structures&rdquo;: &ldquo;Structures without ground forces may maintain control of a planet with coexisting ground forces. Structures without ground forces may coexist by themselves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Structure limits apply to each player separately. A planet in coexistence may contain up to two PDS and one space dock belonging to each player.<sup>1,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 85.4: &ldquo;A player can have a maximum of one space dock on each planet.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 85.5: &ldquo;A player can have a maximum of two PDS units on each planet.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;63.3 does not prevent Titans from placing a PDS on a planet that contains 2 of another player&rsquo;s PDS units. You cannot place a structure on a planet with the maximum number of your structures &hellip;&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If only one player has units on a planet that was in coexistence, and that player was coexisting, they gain control of that planet.<sup>6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;If a player&rsquo;s coexisting units become the only units on a planet, that player gains control of the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence Control Default (With Multiple Coexisting Players).&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;control is exchanged immediately after the coexisting player becomes the only player with units on the planet&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol>
        <li>Outside of the <b>Invasion</b> step of a tactical action, this happens immediately, such as when the controlling player moves all of their units off that planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;During the Invasion step of a tactical action, control is not exchanged until the Establish Control step. At all other times, control is exchanged immediately after the coexisting player becomes the only player with units on the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>During an invasion, if the last of the controlling player&rsquo;s units on a planet are destroyed, such as by <a href="/R_bombardment"><sc>Bombardment</sc></a>, control does not pass to the coexisting player until the <b>Establish Control</b> step. The invading player may land ground forces and gain control of the planet, and the coexisting player remains in coexistence.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexist Control Default (During Invasions)&rdquo;: &ldquo;During invasion, control default due to coexistence does not occur until the establish control step. The invading player may land ground forces and take control of the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;During the Invasion step of a tactical action, control is not exchanged until the Establish Control step&rdquo; Does not state that the coexisting player remains.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>A player who enters coexistence on a planet they control when ground forces are committed to it may still use <a href="/R_space_cannon"><sc>Space Cannon</sc></a> against those ground forces during the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step. They do not lose control of the planet until the <b>Establish Control</b> step, and if they are then the only player with units on the planet, they are no longer coexisting and retain control.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;You may fire PDS defense against the active player&rsquo;s committed ground forces after entering coexistence &hellip; you do not lose control of the planet when you enter coexistence until the Establish Control step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>If multiple players are coexisting on a planet and the player that controls that planet no longer has units on it, control passes to the next coexisting player in the order used to resolve abilities in the current phase: initiative order during the action phase, otherwise clockwise order.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence Control Default (With Multiple Coexisting Players)&rdquo;: &ldquo;Control defaults to the next player in order as per ability resolution order by phase (initiative order during action phase, otherwise clockwise).&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Control resolves to the player next in initiative order (starting with the Active Player) in the Action Phase and in Speaker order in all other phases.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
    <li>All coexisting units are blockaded, regardless of what ships, if any, are in the system. This includes units that are not structures, such as mechs with the <a href="/R_production"><sc>Production</sc></a> ability.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;Coexisting structures are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;coexisting units are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A: &ldquo;Coexisting units &hellip; are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Coexisting structures still work but count as blockaded&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Are coexisting non-structure units with PRODUCTION blockaded? A: Yes, coexisting units are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence and PRODUCTION&rdquo;: &ldquo;Coexisting units can produce ground forces but are always blockaded and cannot produce ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Coexisting units may use their <a href="/R_production"><sc>Production</sc></a> ability to produce ground forces, but cannot produce ships.<sup>9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence and PRODUCTION&rdquo;: &ldquo;Coexisting units can produce ground forces but are always blockaded and cannot produce ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit with <a href="/R_planetary_shield"><sc>Planetary Shield</sc></a> on a planet protects all units on that planet, including those of the controlling player and coexisting players. All PDS on a planet, including coexisting PDS, may use <a href="/R_space_cannon"><sc>Space Cannon</sc></a> during the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step against ground forces committed by the active player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Planetary Shield from any units on the planet applies to the planet itself and thus protects all units on the planet. All PDS on a planet may use Space Cannon during Space Cannon Defense against units committed from space by the active player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player uses the <a href="/R_bombardment"><sc>Bombardment</sc></a> ability of 1 of their units against a planet in coexistence, they choose which player&rsquo;s units on that planet will have the hits assigned.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;When a player makes a bombardment roll against a planet, they choose which of their units with bombardment are bombarding which other player&rsquo;s units, rolling against each player separately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol>
        <li>If that player has multiple units with the <a href="/R_bombardment"><sc>Bombardment</sc></a> ability, they make this choice independently for each unit.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;they choose which of their units with bombardment are bombarding which other player&rsquo;s units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>If that player produces a surplus of hits above the number of units of the player they have chosen, the extra hits cannot be assigned to the units of a different player on that planet.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Same passage: &ldquo;rolling against each player separately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
    <li>A player who controls a planet may start a ground combat against a coexisting player during the invasion step of a tactical action.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;Coexisting units can have combat initiated against them by units being committed to the planet that contains them, including units that they were already coexisting with.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;if you own the planet, or win ground combat against the planet&rsquo;s owner you can attack other players who were already in coexistence at the start of this turn&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who is coexisting on a planet may start a ground combat against the player that controls that planet during the invasion step of a tactical action.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;a coexisting player may choose to end their coexistence by activating the coexisting unit&rsquo;s system and committing the coexisting units against the planet they are coexisting on.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a coexisting player end coexistence and attack?&rdquo;: &ldquo;activate the system and commit the coexisting units against the planet; win the ground combat and you take control as normal&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who had no units on a planet, but commits 1 or more ground forces during the invasion step of a tactical action must start a ground combat against the player that controls that planet (unless they have an ability that would allow them to coexist).<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;When a unit or units coexist with another player&rsquo;s units, they do not trigger combat.&rdquo; Only units with a coexist effect avoid combat.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Should a player win the ground combat on a planet resulting from one of these aforementioned methods, they gain control of that planet, if they were not in control of it already. Then, they may start an additional ground combat against another coexisting player, if present, and may continue to do so until they decline to start another ground combat or there are no more coexisting players. Any coexisting players that they do not resolve a ground combat against remain coexisting.<sup>7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;If a player does wish to engage coexisting units, they may optionally engage a coexisting player&rsquo;s units after winning a combat against the planet&rsquo;s owner, initiating separate combats against any coexisting player as desired.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player ends their coexistence by starting a ground combat, all of their units on that planet, including structures, stop coexisting, even if they do not win the ground combat.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;When you end coexistence, you end coexistence for all of your units on that planet, including structures.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When scoring an objective, a coexisting player considers both themselves and the controlling player to control that planet. The player that controls the planet does not count a coexisting player as also controlling it.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Yes, coexist&rsquo;s control counts for any rule regarding &lsquo;scoring objectives&rsquo;.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence, Perspective During Scoring&rdquo;: &ldquo;the coexisting player sees both themselves and the controlling player as controlling that planet when scoring objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;When scoring objectives&rdquo; a coexisting player counts as controlling the planet.</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they count as controlling it for objectives and neighbors&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence, Perspective During Scoring&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only the coexisting player counts themselves as controlling that planet. The player the coexisting player is coexisting with does not count the coexisting player as also controlling that planet.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Control via coexistence is only seen by the player coexisting, so the other players see 3 planets controlled&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol><li>A player may score public objectives even if they coexist on, but do not control, each planet in their home system.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Scoring Public Objectives Coexisting At Home&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, you may score public objectives even if you coexist on but do not outright control each planet in your home system.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can a player score public objectives if they coexist in their own home system? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li></ol>
    <li>A player must control a planet, not merely coexist on it, to explore it.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexist and Exploration&rdquo;: &ldquo;You must control a planet outright to explore it.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;You cannot explore a planet owned by another player&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot spend another player&rsquo;s coexisting units to pay the cost of an exploration card, such as <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Exploration#Hazardous_Exploration_Cards:~:text=Volatile%20Fuel%20Source"><i>Volatile Fuel Source</i></a>.<sup>9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Same answer: &ldquo;You cannot spend other player&rsquo;s units to pay for explore cards&rsquo; costs.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
</article>
